{
  "schema": "ld-s10y-image/figure-spec@2",
  "id": "fig-141",
  "mode": "deterministic",
  "description": "One shared spherical-mirror frame: center O, vertex P, secondary-axis contact K, schematic paraxial focus F, radius R and FP=R/2, with the perpendicular focal-plane trace. The focus and focal plane retain the approved approximate scope.",
  "source": {
    "image": {
      "path": "ssot-resources/soviet10year-textbooks/artifacts/10p/figures/fig-141.png",
      "sha256": "a25842f75b6e7818f877566158ccfad4a7324789fc4c646aef5d0b482324c49b"
    },
    "authoritativeText": [
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "A smooth curved surface can also reflect light and form an **image**.\nExamples include reflective glass ornaments, convex mirrors at road junctions, and spherical mirrors in some telescopes.\n\nA plane mirror forms an image the same size as the object, whereas a curved reflecting surface can form a larger or smaller image.\nThe image position also need not follow the plane-mirror rule of equal object and image distances from the mirror.\nThis lesson considers one kind of curved reflecting surface: the **spherical mirror**.\n\nModern clarification: We describe light propagation with rays in geometrical optics and treat reflection from the smooth mirror as specular reflection.\nWhen a beam is later treated as meeting at one point, the small-ray-height and small-angle conditions must be retained; the model is not exact for all rays."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "A mirror whose reflecting surface is part of a sphere is called a **spherical mirror**.\nThe center of that sphere is called the mirror's optical center in the source; it is point $O$ in Figure 141.\n\nModern clarification: The usual modern term is **center of curvature**, meaning the center of the sphere that defines the mirror surface.\nThis optical center is not the optical center used for approximately undeviated rays in the paraxial thin-lens model; a spherical mirror reflects light at its surface."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "Figure 141"
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "The point at the middle of the mirror surface is the **vertex**, labeled $P$ in the figure.\nIn the source's terminology, a line through the center of curvature is an **optical axis** of the mirror, such as $OP$ or $OK$."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "phy10-c2-s2-n70",
        "text": "Among these axes, the line $OP$ through the center of curvature and the vertex is called the **principal axis**.\nWhat distinguishes it is that it is also the symmetry axis of the symmetric spherical mirror considered here.\nThe other optical axes through the center of curvature are called **secondary axes**."
      }
    ],
